WI-ginuj. `--full` collected packages/* and never the repo-root tests/,
while CI runs the committed manifest, which does include root tests. So a
green local "full" was not a superset of what CI runs and did not predict
it: PR #890 went green locally over 26,702 tests and red in CI on
test_adr_readme_index_sync.py, a test the local gate holds and did not run.

FIVE SITES, NOT THE THREE FILED. The item found them by grepping
run_pytest; kick_off_background_suite calls pytest directly and
run_full_suite's MANIFEST WRITER enumerates with find, so two were
invisible to that grep. All five now share one definition, and a
structural test fails on any sixth site that spells the target out -- the
control that would have found the two the grep missed.

THE REASON THIS WAS DEFERRED WAS THE WRONG REASON. The risk on file was
COV_PATHS. Coverage is measured over packages/*/src, so more tests can
only raise it; the combined run just went green at 100% over 69,873
statements. The real blocker is a BASENAME COLLISION: tests/
test_generate_concepts.py and packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/
test_generate_concepts.py, which under pytest's default import mode makes
one run raise "import file mismatch" and INTERRUPT COLLECTION. Verified
with --collect-only. The core file is renamed to match its own docstring
and a guard test keeps the two suites' basenames disjoint.

VALIDATED, NOT ASSUMED: ./scripts/smart-test --full now runs 29,065
passed / 8 skipped / 15 xfailed in 21m34s at 100% coverage, against
26,702 before.

FIX (b), the half that survives the next divergence: the suite prints what
it collected and what it did not, derived by asking git for tracked files
and pyproject for pytest's own python_files. Its first real run reported
two directories uncollected; both were false positives of a glob looser
than pytest's rule -- top_level_test_map.py, test_masking.py and
test_only_reachability.py are production modules whose names merely
contain the word. A disclosure that cries wolf every run is one nobody
reads.

The disclosure's first cut passed `bash -n` and died at RUNTIME: a `)` at
column 0 inside its heredoc closed the surrounding $() early, and $() is
re-parsed lazily so the static check never saw it. The behavioural test
caught what the syntax check could not, which is why that test drives the
function rather than reading it.

